1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
*music*

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,980
Well, good morning. You guys having a good morning? My son was not in my house last night, so I slept like a baby. Feeling pretty energetic this morning, excited to be here. If we haven't had the privilege of meeting yet, my name is Ryan and I have the privilege of being one of the pastors here at

3
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:29,960
Canvas Community Church, where we believe inviting real people with real lives into a Christ-centered community. And today we are wrapping up a two-part series that we've done over the last eight weeks on the practice, the spiritual practice of building Christian community and then the spiritual practice of taking the community we've built and leveraging that for service into our community. And so it's been a fun eight weeks. Hopefully you've benefited from that. Hopefully you've worked hard to cultivate more relationships in smaller

4
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:46,960
groups. We talk a lot about getting out of rows and into circles that the church that we read about on the pages of Scripture did not look a whole lot like gathering in an auditorium, all of you facing one person, but it looked a whole lot more like sitting around a table, which we'll talk about more this morning.

5
00:01:46,960 --> 00:02:01,760
So I do want to shout out a group of people that aren't here this morning. I do want to shout out a group of people that aren't here this morning. There are probably 30 to 40 of our most committed women who are at the ladies' retreat this morning. So hopefully we've been praying for them and excited to hear testimonies about what God has been doing in their lives.

6
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:16,860
And also, I get home from church on Sundays, sit down on the couch, and eventually when I get to opening my phone, I always have notifications on Facebook from a few individuals that watch loyally and faithfully online.

7
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:24,020
And so I just wanted to say good morning to you all online. Specifically, Pastor Matt's mom, Joan, is always online.

8
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:33,420
She's always encouraging our community. And so I just, for whatever reason this morning, I felt like the Lord wanted me to say that we're glad you're part of our community, even though you can't make it in person.

9
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:41,760
So, before I pray, I do want to briefly recap this last portion of the series we've been in with the idea of service.

10
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:50,540
But I want to set it in its proper context, because I don't know that I've done the best job of communicating this over the past eight weeks.

11
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:56,700
But this series isn't just a, it's not just a theology about Christian service.

12
00:02:56,700 --> 00:03:01,700
It's not an overview of all the things the Bible has to say about Christian service.

13
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,500
It's built on this series, Practicing the Way.

14
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:11,940
And so what we're talking about, the last four weeks when we've been talking about service, we're talking about how to build the practice of service into your life.

15
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:17,700
And so if I was going to give you an overview of everything the Bible had to say about service, I probably, we probably would have structured it differently.

16
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:22,600
But we're structuring it around the idea that we want to be a people who don't just hear the word, but do the word.

17
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:28,700
And so I don't want to just give you an intellectual overview of all the Bible has to say about service.

18
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:36,260
I want to give you building blocks that you can begin to put in your life that will form you into a person that does Christ-like service.

19
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:37,240
Does that make sense?

20
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:50,920
All right, so this is kind of the building blocks, the steps that we've said that you can begin to put into your life that I believe will, by the power of the Holy Spirit, transform you into a person of service.

21
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:57,220
Again, because doing service apart from being a person who does service is not the point.

22
00:03:57,220 --> 00:04:00,620
We can go out and do religious activity and miss the point.

23
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:07,620
So the first week we said that the practice of service starts first and foremost with cultivating a heart of service.

24
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:11,660
That it has to be a fruit that comes out of us, not just an action that we do.

25
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:18,120
We have to spend time alone by ourselves with the Lord, reminding ourselves of how the Lord has served us.

26
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:23,140
If we are ever going to be a people that serve out of the overflow of what Jesus has done for us,

27
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:31,360
we first and foremost have to start by cultivating that fruit by the power of the Holy Spirit, spending time with the one who has served us.

28
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:41,820
And then we move to serving those close to us in private because we said that service is one of those things where it's really easy to do the right thing for the wrong reasons.

29
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:50,120
And if you don't have this mature heart, if you don't have this place where you're not serving for accolades and for recognition,

30
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:57,180
you can easily find yourself out over your skis serving in public and turning all of the glory that's supposed to be God's back on yourself.

31
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:05,540
And so we serve those close to us in private first so that we cultivate this heart of doing the right things for the right reasons.

32
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:08,160
And then we move out towards the public.

33
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:11,060
We move out towards serving those that are far from us.

34
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:19,340
And we do so, we can do so out of a heart for just these people, a burden for these people, not trying to get credit for anything,

35
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:22,800
but just doing the thing that Jesus told us to do.

36
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:30,000
And today we're really just going to recap kind of the last eight weeks, putting community and service as a practice together.

37
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:35,960
And we're going to have a message on how the goal of serving others really is about cultivating the family of God,

38
00:05:36,020 --> 00:05:37,940
both inside and outside the church.

39
00:05:37,940 --> 00:05:40,700
So that's where community and service come together.

40
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:43,760
Community is about cultivating the family of God inside the church.

41
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:48,760
And service is about going out to the margins, going out to people who don't have a family,

42
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:52,920
who are separated from God and bringing them into the family of God.

43
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:57,860
It really starts in this private moment where you're cultivating a heart with God,

44
00:05:58,020 --> 00:06:03,540
and then you're going outward and outward and outward until you're going out and you're grabbing people who are far from God

45
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:08,380
and you are loving them and serving them and you're inviting them to come back to the family of God.

46
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:09,220
Does that make sense?

47
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:11,880
All right, so that's where we're headed this morning.

48
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:16,100
Let's pray and we will, I don't like to say get started because we already started.

49
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:17,440
We will continue.

50
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:23,740
Father in heaven, God, you are Abba.

51
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:24,940
Father in heaven, God, you are our daddy.

52
00:06:24,940 --> 00:06:32,260
And we, oftentimes I lose sight of this family imagery.

53
00:06:32,260 --> 00:06:35,360
God, that you've invited me into your family.

54
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:42,500
And as your son, I am deeply loved.

55
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:48,300
And everyone in this room who has come under the lordship of Jesus has been adopted into the family of God.

56
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:52,340
They are sons and daughters and they are deeply, deeply loved.

57
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:55,020
And there is nothing they can do to have that taken away from them.

58
00:06:55,020 --> 00:07:01,220
And those of us who have received that desperately want those of us, those who haven't yet received it,

59
00:07:01,220 --> 00:07:06,100
to taste and see that the Lord is good and that they have a papa, they have an Abba in heaven

60
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:10,460
who loves them and who wants them to sit at the table of the family of God.

61
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:16,980
And so God, for those of us who have tasted and seen, for those of us who have a seat at the table in God's family,

62
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:22,720
may we be a people who cultivate living like we are family members of God.

63
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:31,800
And as we cultivate that, may we go out and love others who are not yet at the table and welcome them to the table for your glory,

64
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,920
for our good, and for the good of our neighbors.

65
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:35,500
In Jesus' name I pray.

66
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:36,340
Amen.

67
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:43,660
If you want to follow along with me, we're pretty much going to be covering one verse this morning in Romans chapter 12, verse 13.

68
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:48,160
It'll be on the screen if you don't have a Bible, but if you want to look around at the context while I'm talking

69
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:51,820
and play around with the text yourself, you can do that.

70
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:56,680
A few years ago, actually, it's probably more than a few years ago now.

71
00:07:56,680 --> 00:08:01,500
It feels like a few years ago, but Marissa and I saved up some money and leveraged some credit card points.

72
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:03,900
Don't hit me, Dave Ramsey people, don't do it, all right?

73
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:10,480
I don't carry a balance, but, you know, we leveraged some credit card points, and I went on my first ever cruise.

74
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:11,820
How many cruise people are in the room?

75
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:14,960
All right, cruise people are pretty passionate about cruises.

76
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:15,900
I've only been on one.

77
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:19,560
I really enjoyed it, but it was a lovely experience.

78
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:24,660
Lots of sitting by pools, singing karaoke, winning the shipwide bocce ball tournament.

79
00:08:24,660 --> 00:08:26,300
I know, thank you, thank you.

80
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:30,160
There was like seven people that showed up to it, but I won.

81
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:33,780
It was awesome, and I ate lots and lots of cheeseburgers.

82
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:37,560
It was a week of relaxation where I didn't have to think about anything.

83
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:38,700
That's the thing I love about cruises.

84
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:39,660
You don't have to think about anything.

85
00:08:39,660 --> 00:08:41,240
It's all done for you.

86
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:44,820
Like, literally, you just sit and the place moves.

87
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:46,360
You're sitting on a moving thing.

88
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:48,700
It's moving to the place that you need to go to.

89
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:49,620
You don't have to do anything.

90
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:55,140
And so I just really didn't have to think about anything at all,

91
00:08:55,140 --> 00:08:58,820
and there was one particular thing that I guarantee you I was not thinking about.

92
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:00,000
I think we have a picture of it.

93
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,660
This is the rudder of the ship.

94
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:07,960
I can tell you with certainty that the entire week I was on the cruise ship,

95
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:11,520
I did not think about the rudder of the ship one single time.

96
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:17,180
Seemingly small and insignificant component of the ship is one of the primary tools that

97
00:09:17,180 --> 00:09:19,540
actually allowed me to have the experience that I had.

98
00:09:19,540 --> 00:09:24,480
I didn't think about it at all because, like, I was just doing my thing, living my life.

99
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:31,460
But if that thing was not functioning, if it was not there, I would not have been able to have the experience I had.

100
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:33,140
The pools and the food were great.

101
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:38,880
But if I found out that this was not there, they quickly would have lost their appeal

102
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:41,100
because we would have been adrift at sea.

103
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:49,140
And I feel like many of us have heard illustrations similar to this where something small and seemingly insignificant can have a tremendous impact.

104
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:54,760
And as I prepared for today's sermon, I was struck by the fact that in many ways,

105
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:58,500
I think this is how the early Christian table functioned.

106
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:05,980
It was the table of the early Christians where they cultivated community and where they sent themselves out on service.

107
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:14,040
That literally turned the world upside down, a table, a meeting place for a meal is something small and simple and feels common.

108
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:20,700
And yet it played a massive role in how the early church lived out the mission of God to the world around them.

109
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:22,100
I heard one scholar say it this way.

110
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:27,100
The early church conquered the Roman world, not with swords, but with a table.

111
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:40,660
And I think that there's something that we can recapture in this idea that we as a community of believers are called together around a place of fellowship, a place of communion.

112
00:10:40,660 --> 00:10:44,940
And as we cultivate the values of the kingdom of God in small settings,

113
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:50,960
we take those values out to the places that God has sent us on service and on mission to Jesus.

114
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:56,360
And so what I want to do this morning with the rest of our time is I want to look at three specific tables

115
00:10:56,360 --> 00:11:02,740
that I believe helped the early church move people from strangers to neighbors and from neighbors to family.

116
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:06,380
I'll say that again for those of you that like to take notes.

117
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:11,220
That was a real, that was a good point in my outline where you guys were like, man, that was good.

118
00:11:11,220 --> 00:11:13,940
And I didn't hear any mm's or any amens.

119
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:14,820
I didn't hear anything.

120
00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:15,940
I was like, come on, people.

121
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:19,620
Tables have the power to change the world.

122
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:24,420
And I think we're going to look this morning at three tables that helped the early church move from strangers to neighbors

123
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:26,460
and from neighbors to family.

124
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:27,360
Thank you.

125
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:28,380
Thank you.

126
00:11:28,380 --> 00:11:35,120
The table was a primary place where Christians were formed and expressed their values.

127
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:38,520
Another scholar named Alan Street says it this way.

128
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:45,040
Early Christian meals provided the venue for reading Scripture and apostolic correspondence.

129
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:47,940
These gatherings were more than devotional exercises.

130
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:51,860
They were covenantal meals of instruction and identity formation.

131
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:57,660
So my argument here is that these tables, these places where Christians gather together in smaller settings,

132
00:11:57,660 --> 00:12:02,560
it is the place where we learn to actually live out our identity as citizens of the kingdom.

133
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:08,660
Like there's only so much we can do in this room to form us into the image of Jesus.

134
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:14,280
There's only so much we can do in this room where I can point out the areas in which we are being deformed from the image of Jesus

135
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:17,480
and formed into the patterns of the culture of this world.

136
00:12:17,700 --> 00:12:22,140
But it's around a table and especially in the Roman world, right?

137
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:27,960
Like these Roman, these Christians in the Roman world, they were working constantly, long days

138
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:31,680
and everything about their life was ruled by the Roman Empire.

139
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:35,140
It was these meals around the table where they were able to sit back

140
00:12:35,140 --> 00:12:38,740
and they were able to have control over what it was that they valued.

141
00:12:38,740 --> 00:12:41,940
That their values were upside down to the values of the Roman world.

142
00:12:41,940 --> 00:12:47,140
And they were going to live as citizens of the kingdom of God around this table because they were in charge of this table.

143
00:12:47,140 --> 00:12:51,640
Rome was in charge of everything else, but they were in charge of the Christian table.

144
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:56,620
And they were going to live as if the kingdom of value was coming to earth as it is in heaven.

145
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:58,940
So, Romans chapter 12, verse 13.

146
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:02,600
Let me find it.

147
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:03,380
There it is.

148
00:13:03,380 --> 00:13:10,940
Paul says, share with the Lord's people who are in need and practice hospitality.

149
00:13:10,940 --> 00:13:16,740
Share with the Lord's people who are in need and practice hospitality.

150
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:25,700
And it's in this verse where I think we find the first two tables that we are intended to leverage for the kingdom of God on earth as it is in heaven.

151
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:28,820
And I would say the first table is this, the fellowship table.

152
00:13:28,820 --> 00:13:33,800
And you might be thinking to yourself, Ryan, I don't see the word fellowship in this verse anywhere.

153
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:36,260
And if you are thinking that, that's good.

154
00:13:36,260 --> 00:13:41,000
You should be thinking critically about what I'm telling you and make sure that what I'm telling you is actually biblical, right?

155
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:42,900
Thank you.

156
00:13:42,900 --> 00:13:44,260
All right, good, good, good, good.

157
00:13:45,740 --> 00:13:49,500
So, you're right that you would not see the word fellowship anywhere in here.

158
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:53,180
But this word in verse 13, share with the Lord's people who are in need.

159
00:13:53,180 --> 00:13:55,760
The Greek word finds its root in the word koinonia.

160
00:13:55,760 --> 00:14:01,680
And those of you that grow up in church know that koinonia means fellowship.

161
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:02,740
There we go.

162
00:14:02,740 --> 00:14:05,080
And so, it's this idea.

163
00:14:05,080 --> 00:14:10,820
And fellowship is a thing that we do in church really well, right?

164
00:14:10,820 --> 00:14:15,540
We have fellowship halls where we all gather together.

165
00:14:15,740 --> 00:14:17,300
And we get really deep.

166
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:19,140
And we talk about our sin.

167
00:14:19,140 --> 00:14:21,720
And we talk about what the Lord's doing in our life.

168
00:14:21,720 --> 00:14:23,320
And we hold each other accountable.

169
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:31,120
Or do we just talk about sports and the weather and, right?

170
00:14:31,120 --> 00:14:33,540
Chick-fil-A being closed on Sunday.

171
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:34,880
I really want some today.

172
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:35,620
And I can't.

173
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:41,860
And so, there's this idea, this deep connection idea with the word fellowship.

174
00:14:41,860 --> 00:14:47,340
And Kent McKay has done at least two sermons on the idea of fellowship that you can find online.

175
00:14:47,340 --> 00:14:49,420
I would encourage you to do so.

176
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:56,480
But what I want to get at here is that this verse that Paul is saying, share with the Lord's people who are in need.

177
00:14:56,480 --> 00:15:04,480
I don't know about you, but when I read that at first glance, it's like, okay, like, if I have a brother who doesn't have enough money for food, I'm going to feed him.

178
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:06,340
And I'm going to take care of those needs.

179
00:15:06,340 --> 00:15:22,380
But when we look at the idea of koinonia, this word, this deep-rooted word for fellowship, for a sharing participation in, we realize that what Paul is saying here is way more than just giving somebody a handout when they're in need.

180
00:15:22,480 --> 00:15:25,980
Upon further pondering, the point that Paul is trying to get across here is much deeper.

181
00:15:25,980 --> 00:15:33,660
As followers of Jesus, we literally are being invited to have an active participation in the needs of our brothers and sisters.

182
00:15:33,660 --> 00:15:35,860
This is what true fellowship is.

183
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:50,180
This is how we cultivate fellowship around the table, is that you gather together with a group of people, and you as a follower of Jesus, with other followers of Jesus, are committed to actively sharing and participating in the needs of other people.

184
00:15:51,820 --> 00:15:59,540
And again, I don't know about you, but this has not been my experience with things that we call fellowship in the Christian world.

185
00:15:59,540 --> 00:16:09,880
Like, we're literally called to see our brothers and sisters as family, and when your family is in need, you drop everything you have going on to meet those needs.

186
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:16,840
And that is the type of fellowship that Paul is inviting his brothers and sisters in Christ to cultivate around their tables.

187
00:16:16,840 --> 00:16:21,520
As they gather together in smaller settings around tables, which was church for many of these people.

188
00:16:21,820 --> 00:16:26,560
Like I said, church for the first century world wasn't sitting in rows and looking at one person.

189
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:36,460
It was gathering together around a table to break the body of the bread, which represents Christ's body, and to drink the wine, which represents his blood.

190
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:46,840
So, as Christians, when we pursue fellowship, are we pursuing an active participation in the joys and sorrows of our brothers and sisters?

191
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:50,240
In the needs and provisions of our brothers and sisters?

192
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:55,540
Or are we just looking for a fun social hang with people who share the same values as us?

193
00:16:55,540 --> 00:17:07,120
Because I can tell you one thing, and that is fun social hangouts with people who share the same values as you is not what turned the Roman world upside down,

194
00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:09,340
and it will not turn our culture upside down.

195
00:17:10,060 --> 00:17:16,500
We have plenty of places where you can meet together and have fun social get-togethers with people who have shared values as you.

196
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:20,660
That is not the thing that Paul is talking about here.

197
00:17:20,660 --> 00:17:21,700
And the Romans had it, too.

198
00:17:21,700 --> 00:17:26,020
The Romans had these guild meetings where they would gather together with people in their same trade,

199
00:17:26,020 --> 00:17:29,980
with people who looked like them, talked like them, people in their same socioeconomic status.

200
00:17:29,980 --> 00:17:31,500
They had meals around a table.

201
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:32,880
They would sacrifice to gods.

202
00:17:32,880 --> 00:17:35,680
They would do everything the early church was doing.

203
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:44,540
But the thing that made the early church unique is that they brought an extremely diverse group of people together in one family unit around a table.

204
00:17:44,540 --> 00:17:49,700
And that family unit intentionally worked to meet the needs of those around them.

205
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:52,640
It was part of who they were as a family.

206
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:59,140
They brought together the poor, the widow, and the orphan with the wealthy, important person in the culture,

207
00:17:59,140 --> 00:18:02,060
and they all sat together at the same table.

208
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:06,740
Nowhere else in the Roman world would people have seen what was being done around the Christian table.

209
00:18:06,740 --> 00:18:14,200
Individuals from wildly different ethnicities, genders, and economic statuses were sitting together as equal partners at a meal.

210
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:16,080
This was unheard of.

211
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:25,160
And on top of just that image of Jew and Gentile, slave and free, male and female, all gathering together, rich and poor,

212
00:18:25,160 --> 00:18:28,420
all gathering together around one table to share a meal.

213
00:18:28,420 --> 00:18:34,820
On top of that, the leaders of these groups, like Paul, had the audacity to command the wealthy participants

214
00:18:34,820 --> 00:18:40,880
to go out of their way to sacrificially serve and actively participate in the needs of those with lower statuses.

215
00:18:42,540 --> 00:18:43,740
This is fellowship.

216
00:18:43,740 --> 00:18:47,460
This is the fellowship table that Paul is inviting Christians to cultivate.

217
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:57,900
Around the Christian fellowship table, the wealthy Jewish Roman citizen would have had more in common with a Greek slave

218
00:18:57,900 --> 00:19:03,300
who claimed the name of Jesus than a Roman neighbor of the same socioeconomic status.

219
00:19:04,920 --> 00:19:08,480
And my question for us is, is this how we view the kingdom of God?

220
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:14,300
Do we view our brothers and sisters in Christ as family?

221
00:19:14,300 --> 00:19:19,320
That we are going to actively participate in their needs and their joys and their suffering?

222
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:20,640
That when they weep, we weep.

223
00:19:20,640 --> 00:19:22,120
That when they rejoice, we rejoice.

224
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:24,580
Are we cultivating this fellowship table?

225
00:19:24,580 --> 00:19:26,680
And we can't do it with everyone in this room.

226
00:19:26,680 --> 00:19:28,020
This is why we talk about small groups.

227
00:19:28,020 --> 00:19:29,220
This is why we talk about house churches.

228
00:19:29,220 --> 00:19:33,600
This is why we talk about being in life-on-life fellowship with brothers and sisters.

229
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:41,240
But are you cultivating this type of table where you are gathered around a diverse group of people

230
00:19:41,240 --> 00:19:46,660
with different ideologies and thoughts and feelings about the election

231
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:49,720
and different thoughts and feelings about what's going on in this world

232
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:53,160
from different economic statuses and genders and ethnicities?

233
00:19:53,160 --> 00:19:55,660
Are we working towards this?

234
00:19:55,660 --> 00:19:57,620
It won't happen overnight.

235
00:19:57,620 --> 00:19:58,620
It won't be easy.

236
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:04,380
But this is fellowship.

237
00:20:04,380 --> 00:20:08,300
This is the family of God around a table.

238
00:20:08,300 --> 00:20:14,480
And I just can't help but wonder, I heard, Corey and I were talking the other day,

239
00:20:14,480 --> 00:20:16,120
and I think it was him that used this term.

240
00:20:16,120 --> 00:20:17,660
I don't know if he was quoting somebody else.

241
00:20:17,660 --> 00:20:22,200
But in the same way, in the same way that a wealthy Jewish Roman citizen

242
00:20:22,200 --> 00:20:26,280
has more in common with a Greek slave who claims the name of Jesus than their Roman neighbor,

243
00:20:26,280 --> 00:20:30,820
I think we as Christians, we have to acknowledge the fact that we have more in common

244
00:20:30,820 --> 00:20:35,800
with our minority brothers and sisters in Christ who are in places in the world

245
00:20:35,800 --> 00:20:39,800
that are being persecuted for their faith, who don't look like us, don't talk like us,

246
00:20:39,800 --> 00:20:40,600
don't act like us.

247
00:20:40,600 --> 00:20:42,400
They are our brothers and sisters in Christ,

248
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:45,620
and we should have more in common with them than we do with the person,

249
00:20:45,620 --> 00:20:47,960
the atheist that lives right down the street,

250
00:20:47,960 --> 00:20:51,520
that has the same ideology as us, that grew up in the same culture as we do.

251
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:58,520
Like this is fellowship and active participation in the lives of the brothers and sisters in Christ around us.

252
00:20:58,520 --> 00:21:04,480
And imagine for me just one second how attractive this table would have looked

253
00:21:04,480 --> 00:21:09,280
to those outside the church who did not have cultural privilege or power.

254
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:15,320
Imagine if you were a Greek slave who your entire life,

255
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:18,140
the Jews have looked down on you, the Romans have looked down on you,

256
00:21:18,140 --> 00:21:22,600
all day long, every day you were being mistreated, taken advantage of,

257
00:21:22,600 --> 00:21:27,260
viewed as second-class citizen, and you're on your way home after a long day's work,

258
00:21:27,260 --> 00:21:29,120
and you look over and you see a table,

259
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:34,500
and you just keep walking by because you know nobody at that table is gonna look at you

260
00:21:34,500 --> 00:21:36,800
as if that you are an equal partner around the table,

261
00:21:36,800 --> 00:21:39,960
and then you take a second glance and you see a Greek slave

262
00:21:39,960 --> 00:21:44,500
and someone with wealthy status, a male and a female,

263
00:21:44,500 --> 00:21:46,520
and they're all sitting together around a table.

264
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:51,140
Could you imagine what that might have done in the imagination of this person

265
00:21:51,140 --> 00:21:53,460
who has been told their whole lives that they are worth nothing

266
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:58,400
and they are not welcome in any of the places that others are?

267
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:02,240
I don't know about you, but it sounds to me like the early church understood

268
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:05,480
the commission of the Sermon on the Mount a bit better than I do.

269
00:22:05,480 --> 00:22:12,980
Because their community, their fellowship actually looked like the application of the Sermon on the Mount.

270
00:22:13,380 --> 00:22:18,400
Blessed are those who are poor, blessed are those who are needy, blessed are those who are taken advantage of.

271
00:22:18,400 --> 00:22:24,880
This is how they began to live out the values of the kingdom of God that are expressed in the Sermon on the Mount.

272
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:31,000
And I don't know about you, but I look at my fellowship tables, and they don't always look like that.

273
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:40,960
And this is Paul's invitation to us to spend our lives cultivating this type of fellowship

274
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:45,440
that shares with the Lord's people who are in need.

275
00:22:45,440 --> 00:22:50,920
Maybe this passage doesn't mean a whole lot to us because a lot of the people that we are doing life with

276
00:22:50,920 --> 00:22:51,940
don't have many needs.

277
00:22:51,940 --> 00:22:59,540
And maybe the problem with that is we are not viewing the people that God says

278
00:22:59,540 --> 00:23:02,960
are valuable in the kingdom of God as valuable in the kingdom of God.

279
00:23:03,580 --> 00:23:12,940
And this is where community and service come together.

280
00:23:12,940 --> 00:23:25,680
Are we building familial relationships with a diverse group of people across ethnicities and socioeconomic statuses?

281
00:23:25,680 --> 00:23:28,160
Is that something that we are actively pursuing?

282
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:33,620
Are we looking for ways to actively participate in the needs of our brothers and sisters in Christ,

283
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:35,240
physical, emotional, and spiritual?

284
00:23:35,240 --> 00:23:39,400
And here's another big one for us as Americans.

285
00:23:39,400 --> 00:23:45,700
Are we willing to humble ourselves and allow our brothers and sisters to meet our needs?

286
00:23:45,700 --> 00:23:49,820
Are we allowing our brothers and sisters to wash our feet?

287
00:23:49,820 --> 00:23:53,860
And for me, that's one of the most impactful things about the threefold service.

288
00:23:55,080 --> 00:23:59,880
It's not, it's hard, but it's not that hard to get down on my knees and to wash somebody else's feet.

289
00:23:59,880 --> 00:24:05,560
The thing that is hard for me is when somebody taps me on the shoulder and says,

290
00:24:05,560 --> 00:24:07,360
take your shoes off, I'm washing your feet.

291
00:24:07,360 --> 00:24:12,200
And I'm like, no, no thanks.

292
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:15,900
But here's the thing, the math doesn't work there.

293
00:24:15,900 --> 00:24:19,340
You wanna come to the threefold service?

294
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:20,500
You wanna wash people's feet?

295
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:21,900
You don't wanna get your feet washed?

296
00:24:21,900 --> 00:24:24,480
If that was all of us, it'd be a pretty lame service

297
00:24:24,480 --> 00:24:27,440
Twofold.

298
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:28,660
That's right, mom.

299
00:24:28,660 --> 00:24:30,360
It's a twofold service at that point.

300
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:32,120
She taught me math.

301
00:24:32,120 --> 00:24:33,080
I should have got that before.

302
00:24:33,080 --> 00:24:39,680
And this is the Christian table that has the power to turn the world upside down,

303
00:24:39,680 --> 00:24:42,220
where we view each other as family.

304
00:24:42,220 --> 00:24:47,120
And because of that, we do whatever it takes to actively participate in the needs of our brothers and sisters.

305
00:24:49,060 --> 00:24:55,040
And again, this is where the community and the service, they join together.

306
00:24:55,040 --> 00:25:01,640
Because like I said, if our tables are filled with only people who look like us, talk like us, act like us, who don't have that many needs.

307
00:25:03,980 --> 00:25:08,980
Then maybe, just maybe, we're called to do what the second part of this verse says.

308
00:25:08,980 --> 00:25:16,420
And we're called to go out from our communities to people who don't have a family, who are marginalized, who are powerless.

309
00:25:16,420 --> 00:25:19,660
And maybe it's our call to invite them to come into our family.

310
00:25:20,320 --> 00:25:22,280
So that there are needs to be met.

311
00:25:22,280 --> 00:25:29,020
So that we can take the blessings that the Lord has given us and we can serve others in that way.

312
00:25:29,020 --> 00:25:32,500
So verse 13, share with the Lord's people who are in need fellowship table, right?

313
00:25:32,500 --> 00:25:36,500
Like actively participate in the needs of your brothers and sisters in Christ.

314
00:25:36,500 --> 00:25:39,560
This is very clearly a command for Christians and Christians.

315
00:25:39,560 --> 00:25:43,500
It's how brothers and sisters are called to treat one another.

316
00:25:43,840 --> 00:25:50,100
And the second part of the verse, he says, practice hospitality, which gets us to our second table, the host table.

317
00:25:50,100 --> 00:25:59,140
And again, one of those hospitality is one of those words like fellowship, where we've taken it and made it mean something that it doesn't actually mean in the Bible.

318
00:25:59,140 --> 00:26:03,840
When we talk about fellowship, we think about Joanna Gaines, right?

319
00:26:03,840 --> 00:26:05,340
Anybody else?

320
00:26:05,340 --> 00:26:06,360
Or sorry, hospitality.

321
00:26:06,360 --> 00:26:07,900
Talk about hospitality.

322
00:26:07,900 --> 00:26:09,280
Anybody else think about Joanna Gaines?

323
00:26:09,280 --> 00:26:13,600
Anybody else thinking about the Hearth and Heather section in Target?

324
00:26:13,600 --> 00:26:14,360
Am I the only one?

325
00:26:14,360 --> 00:26:16,800
Right?

326
00:26:16,800 --> 00:26:21,660
Oftentimes in Christian circles, we talk about hospitality and it's how Christians treat other Christians.

327
00:26:21,660 --> 00:26:23,420
It's welcoming them into their home.

328
00:26:23,420 --> 00:26:24,880
It's like, hey, the house is clean.

329
00:26:24,880 --> 00:26:26,160
I lit some candles.

330
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:27,900
I got a charcuterie board.

331
00:26:27,900 --> 00:26:28,760
It's going to be great.

332
00:26:28,760 --> 00:26:29,440
We're going to sit around.

333
00:26:29,440 --> 00:26:30,380
We're going to talk about Jesus.

334
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:32,440
I got the gift of hospitality.

335
00:26:32,440 --> 00:26:37,200
That's not what the Bible means when it says hospitality.

336
00:26:37,200 --> 00:26:41,540
The Greek word for hospitality is actually phyloxenia.

337
00:26:41,540 --> 00:26:43,240
Literally translated.

338
00:26:43,360 --> 00:26:45,440
It means love of stranger.

339
00:26:45,440 --> 00:26:54,060
So Paul is very clearly moving from a command for Christians to treat other Christians by actively participating in their needs of others.

340
00:26:54,720 --> 00:26:59,660
And he moves from Christians treating Christians to how Christians are called to treat strangers.

341
00:26:59,660 --> 00:27:03,400
This is people outside of the family of God.

342
00:27:03,400 --> 00:27:06,900
Paul says we have a priority to take care of one another within the household of God.

343
00:27:06,900 --> 00:27:08,100
Starts with us.

344
00:27:08,100 --> 00:27:10,540
There should never be needs among us in the household of God.

345
00:27:10,680 --> 00:27:13,360
But as we are meeting the needs of others in the household of God.

346
00:27:13,360 --> 00:27:24,760
On top of that, we need to be going out from this table, from the fellowship table, out and creating a table of hospitality that welcomes people who are outside of the family of God to the family of God with no strings attached.

347
00:27:26,200 --> 00:27:30,140
This also isn't just about a handout that meets a temporary physical need.

348
00:27:30,140 --> 00:27:35,280
It's literally familial love of stranger.

349
00:27:36,020 --> 00:27:43,000
If you're loving a stranger like family, it doesn't mean you're going to go out and give them a cup of soup and walk away and not even ask their name.

350
00:27:44,760 --> 00:27:50,780
Love of stranger, family love of stranger means cultivating a heart where you treat strangers like family.

351
00:27:50,780 --> 00:27:53,920
This is the kingdom of God.

352
00:27:53,920 --> 00:27:56,420
This is the invitation of the Christian.

353
00:27:56,420 --> 00:28:01,440
It's not about meeting the physical need only.

354
00:28:01,440 --> 00:28:06,800
It's about cultivating a loving relationship that treats these image bearers of God with dignity and worth.

355
00:28:06,800 --> 00:28:10,020
It means we meet practical and relational needs.

356
00:28:10,020 --> 00:28:13,720
Christian hospitality looks less like Joanna Gaines and more like Mother Teresa.

357
00:28:14,760 --> 00:28:19,880
This is what we are called to cultivate as Christians in service.

358
00:28:19,880 --> 00:28:23,360
So we don't just go to the soup kitchen once a month.

359
00:28:23,360 --> 00:28:30,660
We go to the soup kitchen and we sit down across the table from those we just served and we ask them their name.

360
00:28:30,660 --> 00:28:33,040
And we ask them their story.

361
00:28:33,040 --> 00:28:34,600
And we just listen.

362
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:43,400
We have this thing, I don't know, I feel like church culture, we have this way of thinking about people inside the church and people outside the church.

363
00:28:44,760 --> 00:28:52,500
And hospitality is, we're called to view those outside the church as people that God wants to sit down at the fellowship table.

364
00:28:52,500 --> 00:28:58,540
Like the goal of the hospitality table is to move people from the hospitality table to the fellowship table.

365
00:28:58,540 --> 00:29:04,240
It's to treat them like family with no strings attached, inviting them to sit down at the family table.

366
00:29:04,320 --> 00:29:08,240
They are welcome here through the body and blood of Jesus.

367
00:29:08,240 --> 00:29:15,780
And much of Christian service that I have seen in the past is primarily transactional, it's not relational.

368
00:29:15,780 --> 00:29:21,060
Especially Christian service to those outside of the church.

369
00:29:21,060 --> 00:29:23,580
We go and we meet a need and we walk away.

370
00:29:24,700 --> 00:29:32,960
And what Paul here is inviting us to around the table of hospitality is not just relational, or it's not just transactional, it's relational.

371
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:39,040
It's not service provider, service recipient relationship.

372
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:44,780
And I think a lot of times that's how we treat service to those outside the church.

373
00:29:44,780 --> 00:29:49,000
I'm coming to serve you, you're gonna receive service for me, I'm gonna meet a need for you,

374
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:53,820
and then I'm gonna move on and forget about you until I sign up for my next Christian service project.

375
00:29:53,820 --> 00:29:58,880
And what we're invited to is much more than that.

376
00:29:58,880 --> 00:30:03,460
It's not transactional, it's relational.

377
00:30:03,460 --> 00:30:07,940
It's not out of a heart of duty, but it's out of a heart of love.

378
00:30:07,940 --> 00:30:13,200
The goal is not just meeting a physical need, the goal is relationship.

379
00:30:13,660 --> 00:30:20,440
And I don't know how this is sitting with all of you, but this is not how I have viewed my Christian service for most of my life.

380
00:30:20,440 --> 00:30:22,700
And I'm not crushing it right now.

381
00:30:22,700 --> 00:30:29,900
And so if this is heavy on you, it is heavy on me, but I'm just telling you that this is the invitation that changes the world, that turns the world upside down.

382
00:30:29,900 --> 00:30:36,500
When we actively participate in the needs of our brothers and sisters in Christ, when we view them as family,

383
00:30:38,140 --> 00:30:43,520
when the world around us looks at us and says, you guys are weird, why do you do that?

384
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:44,360
Why do you live that way?

385
00:30:44,360 --> 00:30:45,100
It's because we're family.

386
00:30:45,100 --> 00:30:55,940
And then we cultivate this prayerful, intentional, interruptible life where we move from the fellowship table out to those outside the family of God,

387
00:30:55,940 --> 00:31:00,740
and we just keep inviting them into the family of God, making space for them, meeting their needs.

388
00:31:00,740 --> 00:31:02,740
Again, no strings attached.

389
00:31:02,740 --> 00:31:07,180
And I will say, we've got Watts, our Watts week coming up in a few weeks.

390
00:31:07,180 --> 00:31:14,100
And I, a lot of you, I've served with you at Watts, and many of you are really, really good at this.

391
00:31:14,100 --> 00:31:22,220
Like when I come to see, when I come to eat dinner with Watts, it's like most of our people are sitting around the table playing card games

392
00:31:22,220 --> 00:31:26,700
and getting to know these people's stories and investing in relationships with them.

393
00:31:27,780 --> 00:31:30,280
That is what hospitality looks like.

394
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:33,480
It's not just, here's some food, I'll see you later, have a good night, stay warm.

395
00:31:33,480 --> 00:31:38,200
It's, here's some food, tell me your name, tell me your story.

396
00:31:38,200 --> 00:31:40,120
How can I support you?

397
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:41,060
How can I encourage you?

398
00:31:41,060 --> 00:31:41,840
How can I help you?

399
00:31:41,840 --> 00:31:45,900
And obviously, we can't help every single person with every single need like family,

400
00:31:45,900 --> 00:31:51,240
but we've got to be led by the Holy Spirit to see people with dignity and respect to treat them like image bearers.

401
00:31:55,240 --> 00:31:57,240
Holmes, I don't think she's here today.

402
00:31:57,240 --> 00:32:00,980
At Watts, she sets up, she does manicures.

403
00:32:00,980 --> 00:32:09,080
And it's so awesome to just watch her love on these people and literally just like treat them like family.

404
00:32:09,080 --> 00:32:19,360
This is biblical hospitality, treating people outside the family of God with physical, emotional, spiritual needs as family, no strings attached.

405
00:32:20,840 --> 00:32:23,820
And I think we just make it way more complicated than it needs to be.

406
00:32:23,820 --> 00:32:27,640
I know for me, I make it way more complicated than it needs to be.

407
00:32:27,640 --> 00:32:30,020
What does Christian hospitality look like?

408
00:32:30,020 --> 00:32:34,240
It looks like start, just start loving your non-Christian neighbor with no strings attached.

409
00:32:34,240 --> 00:32:43,240
Don't feel like your relationship with them is to just be leveraged to try and change their mind or convince them to receive the gospel of Jesus.

410
00:32:43,240 --> 00:32:45,560
Like just love them with no strings attached.

411
00:32:45,560 --> 00:32:49,520
Ask the dad on the fence beside you at your kids' sporting games how their week was.

412
00:32:49,520 --> 00:32:56,760
And just see, just create space for them to be like, hey, people don't, nobody's asked me how my week was.

413
00:32:56,760 --> 00:33:03,840
The next time you stop to give someone on the street a few dollars or a warm meal, ask them what their name is.

414
00:33:03,840 --> 00:33:06,280
Look them in the eye and ask them how their day is going.

415
00:33:06,280 --> 00:33:09,440
Ask them if there's anything you can pray for them about.

416
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:17,740
Just meet a physical need, see a person, open up the opportunity to meet a spiritual need.

417
00:33:17,740 --> 00:33:24,360
You will be surprised at the seed of relationship that will be planted by just a little unexpected kindness.

418
00:33:24,360 --> 00:33:38,040
Now, it is important, I think, at this point of the message that I do say that as Christians, our ultimate goal isn't just to meet physical needs and build relationships.

419
00:33:38,040 --> 00:33:40,400
Our goal is to bring them back to the table.

420
00:33:40,400 --> 00:33:42,740
Our goal is to proclaim the gospel.

421
00:33:42,740 --> 00:33:50,640
We could swing the pendulum too far where all we ever do is build relationships and meet needs and never tell them about their desperate need for King Jesus.

422
00:33:50,640 --> 00:33:55,460
Never tell them how much better the fellowship table is than the hospitality table.

423
00:33:55,460 --> 00:34:01,040
Never tell them that we've blessed them in this way, but they're missing out on something much deeper.

424
00:34:01,040 --> 00:34:06,740
Never tell them that the hospitality table only lasts as long as Jesus waits to come back.

425
00:34:07,380 --> 00:34:11,300
But when Jesus returns, the only thing that will remain is the fellowship table.

426
00:34:11,300 --> 00:34:19,040
So we want people to stand up from the hospitality table and come to saving knowledge of faith in Jesus and join the fellowship table.

427
00:34:19,040 --> 00:34:20,180
That is our goal.

428
00:34:20,180 --> 00:34:25,600
I heard somebody say this week, hospitality is the bridge by which we earn the right to proclaim the gospel.

429
00:34:26,600 --> 00:34:28,360
That this is our goal.

430
00:34:28,360 --> 00:34:41,380
We first, we love, no strings attached, we meet needs, we build relationships, but we also, because of the relationships we build, we leverage those relationships to say, let me tell you about the hope that I have in Jesus because the grave is empty.

431
00:34:42,180 --> 00:34:45,760
And there is no power of death that can hold us down.

432
00:34:45,760 --> 00:34:53,700
And I know that you probably are worried about death because anybody who doesn't have the hope of an empty grave thinks about death at some point in their life.

433
00:34:53,700 --> 00:35:08,020
And if you want to overcome that fear, let me tell you about a God who came and dwelt in human form and died on the place, on our place for our sins and rose again from the dead, leaving the grave empty so that we don't have to fear death anymore.

434
00:35:09,160 --> 00:35:11,140
You are welcome into this family.

435
00:35:11,140 --> 00:35:17,480
Jesus is our brother, God is our father, and you are welcome to move from the host table to the fellowship table.

436
00:35:17,480 --> 00:35:23,800
This doesn't mean we only get to serve people until we get the chance to tell them about Jesus.

437
00:35:23,800 --> 00:35:28,840
We're not bait and switching people like, hey, I love you, I love you, I love you.

438
00:35:28,840 --> 00:35:29,680
Let me tell you about Jesus.

439
00:35:29,680 --> 00:35:30,500
Oh, you don't want Jesus?

440
00:35:30,500 --> 00:35:31,260
All right, never mind.

441
00:35:31,260 --> 00:35:31,780
See you later.

442
00:35:31,780 --> 00:35:32,740
Right?

443
00:35:32,740 --> 00:35:36,440
Like this is a persistent, intentional relationship building.

444
00:35:39,160 --> 00:35:47,220
We just never stop inviting them to move from the host table to the fellowship table, to be adopted into the family of God.

445
00:35:47,220 --> 00:35:52,420
One of my favorite stories of this at work is that of Rosaria Butterfield.

446
00:35:52,420 --> 00:35:56,380
She is the author of the book, The Gospel Comes with a House Key.

447
00:35:56,380 --> 00:36:04,940
If you've never read this book and you're interested in this idea of loving strangers as neighbors and turning neighbors to friends, you have to read this book.

448
00:36:04,940 --> 00:36:05,980
It is an incredible book.

449
00:36:06,880 --> 00:36:09,240
Rosaria was a tenured professor at a liberal college.

450
00:36:09,240 --> 00:36:14,520
She was an outspoken feminist and lesbian who wrote an article critiquing Christians.

451
00:36:14,520 --> 00:36:21,560
And the local pastor in her town read the article and wrote an opposition article.

452
00:36:21,560 --> 00:36:24,280
No, that's not what he did.

453
00:36:24,280 --> 00:36:25,700
Just kidding.

454
00:36:25,700 --> 00:36:26,080
Gotcha.

455
00:36:26,080 --> 00:36:31,380
He wrote Rosaria a letter inviting her to his house for dinner.

456
00:36:33,880 --> 00:36:40,600
And she accepted as part of what she says, her social research on the radical right.

457
00:36:40,600 --> 00:36:44,840
She was like, all right, I'll go to dinner with you guys, but I'm taking my journal.

458
00:36:44,840 --> 00:36:48,780
I'm going to take notes for my next article about how weird and evil and wicked you guys are.

459
00:36:50,500 --> 00:36:59,060
And she shares her story about how slowly over time this family's kindness and their continued welcoming her into her home, or into their home.

460
00:36:59,060 --> 00:37:11,240
Their continued hospitality, love of stranger, with no strings attached, became the thing that God used to bring her to saving faith in Jesus and move her from the host table to the fellowship table.

461
00:37:12,240 --> 00:37:16,960
And since then, her life has literally been built around this practice of hospitality.

462
00:37:16,960 --> 00:37:22,660
She's going to invite people into her house the same way that she was invited into the house of this pastor.

463
00:37:22,660 --> 00:37:28,960
Reminds me of the verse in Romans that it's God's kindness that leads to repentance.

464
00:37:31,040 --> 00:37:39,600
And I think oftentimes the way that God communicates the gospel to us in a way that can be received is through kindness.

465
00:37:39,600 --> 00:37:46,180
That it's our kindness that introduces others to God's kindness that leads to repentance.

466
00:37:47,600 --> 00:37:55,980
And I think we've got to learn to proclaim the gospel in such a way that with our lives and our words, it could be described as kindness.

467
00:37:55,980 --> 00:38:03,620
And I know a lot of this is heavy, and it could all seem overwhelming.

468
00:38:03,620 --> 00:38:06,160
Again, like my Waffle House menu last week, if you were here.

469
00:38:06,160 --> 00:38:09,580
You're not called to do everything.

470
00:38:09,580 --> 00:38:12,420
You're not called to welcome every stranger into your home.

471
00:38:13,920 --> 00:38:18,860
But don't let the overwhelming nature of all the needs of the world keep you from doing something.

472
00:38:18,860 --> 00:38:23,060
This week, Holy Spirit, who do you want me to show hospitality to?

473
00:38:23,060 --> 00:38:28,160
This week, Holy Spirit, who are you inviting me to cultivate fellowship with?

474
00:38:28,160 --> 00:38:33,920
Those that I already have fellowship with, Lord, what needs might they have that I can meet?

475
00:38:33,920 --> 00:38:40,280
And if none of us have any needs, how can we find others in our community that have needs and meet those needs?

476
00:38:41,140 --> 00:38:46,020
Holy Spirit, what might you be inviting me to do in this area of fellowship and hospitality?

477
00:38:46,020 --> 00:38:50,380
I kind of summarize my message with this main point.

478
00:38:50,380 --> 00:38:56,620
Christian service builds up the family of God through fellowship and welcomes the stranger through hospitality.

479
00:38:56,620 --> 00:38:58,520
This is what Paul is saying in Romans.

480
00:38:58,520 --> 00:39:00,880
Take care of the needs of those in your family.

481
00:39:00,880 --> 00:39:05,160
Actively participate in the needs of those that are brothers and sisters in Christ.

482
00:39:05,160 --> 00:39:12,080
And welcome the stranger into the family of God through the hospitality table, which leads us to our third and final table.

483
00:39:12,080 --> 00:39:14,080
You might have guessed it.

484
00:39:14,080 --> 00:39:16,300
The communion table.

485
00:39:16,300 --> 00:39:23,360
The communion table is the table that makes the fellowship table and the host table possible.

486
00:39:24,080 --> 00:39:29,920
If it was not for Christ serving us in this way, we would not be able to serve others in this way.

487
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:36,200
If it was not for Christ, all service done by us would be out of religious duty.

488
00:39:36,200 --> 00:39:42,080
It would be pulling up our bootstraps and mortifying our flesh and serving others.

489
00:39:42,080 --> 00:39:50,840
But because of Jesus, it is out of the overflow of a full heart that has received the hospitality of Jesus and been welcomed to the fellowship table of Jesus.

490
00:39:50,840 --> 00:39:59,320
It is because of what Jesus has done that we are able to, by the power of the Holy Spirit, go and do this radical command that Paul gave in Romans chapter 12.

491
00:40:03,660 --> 00:40:08,840
Christ's body, broken, his shed blood, brings strangers together into family.

492
00:40:08,840 --> 00:40:13,380
And it motivates that family to actively participate in the suffering of others.

493
00:40:13,380 --> 00:40:19,580
Encourages us to go out and sacrificially love the stranger with the hopes that they will become family as well.

494
00:40:19,580 --> 00:40:24,680
When we participate in the Lord's Supper, we look back to what Christ has done in the past.

495
00:40:26,120 --> 00:40:31,520
We allow it to form us into a people that look like him in the present.

496
00:40:31,520 --> 00:40:37,060
As we await the day when he will fulfill all of his promises in the future.

497
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:43,100
I pulled this quote from a blog.

498
00:40:43,100 --> 00:40:47,380
I think it's a good summary of everything I've just shared.

499
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:54,100
It says, our Savior chose to leave his home in heaven to become a stranger without a place to call his own.

500
00:40:55,180 --> 00:41:02,460
He was not recognized or accepted by his own and was cast outside the city walls to be forsaken and suffer for sinners.

501
00:41:02,460 --> 00:41:12,000
Through his sinless life and sacrifice on the cross, he made it possible for us, who were once enemies of God, to be made holy and accepted by him.

502
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:20,940
And with the promise that he is preparing a place for us, he will come back to take us to our final home to be with him forever.

503
00:41:22,580 --> 00:41:27,540
The ultimate example of hospitality that leads to fellowship is Jesus.

504
00:41:27,540 --> 00:41:31,080
Does our service look like his?

505
00:41:31,080 --> 00:41:38,120
Are we serving people in a way that makes strangers friends and friends family?

506
00:41:40,300 --> 00:41:45,340
If not, may the communion table this morning be a corrective encouragement.

507
00:41:45,340 --> 00:41:57,520
May his body broken for us and his blood shed for us remind us of what it truly means to be citizens of the kingdom of God.

508
00:41:57,520 --> 00:42:02,260
To be brought from strangers to friends and from friends to family.

509
00:42:04,260 --> 00:42:06,800
May we treat each other as family inside the house.

510
00:42:06,800 --> 00:42:14,460
And may we, like Jesus, go outside of the house to meet the needs of others.

511
00:42:14,460 --> 00:42:16,200
To see them.

512
00:42:16,200 --> 00:42:17,340
To love them.

513
00:42:17,340 --> 00:42:20,260
Love them like family, no strings attached.

514
00:42:20,260 --> 00:42:25,840
Inviting them, begging them to come back and sit at the table with Jesus at the head.

515
00:42:25,900 --> 00:42:26,220
Let's pray.

516
00:42:26,220 --> 00:42:38,360
Father in heaven, thank you so much for yet another opportunity to just spend time reflecting on who you are to us and what you've done for us and what you've invited us to do for others.

517
00:42:43,040 --> 00:42:46,680
God, our lives are busy, stressed, we're overwhelmed.

518
00:42:46,680 --> 00:42:56,760
We might be tempted to think, that's not for me, that's for somebody else.

519
00:42:56,760 --> 00:43:05,260
Or maybe when life settles down, but God, may today be the day that many of us are thrust into this application.

520
00:43:05,500 --> 00:43:11,960
That we actively cultivate family around the table with brothers and sisters in Christ.

521
00:43:11,960 --> 00:43:14,980
That we actively participate in their needs and their suffering.

522
00:43:14,980 --> 00:43:23,800
May we be a people who look for opportunities to treat strangers like friends and invite friends to become family.

523
00:43:23,800 --> 00:43:27,460
Jesus' name I pray.

524
00:43:27,460 --> 00:43:28,580
Amen.

525
00:43:28,580 --> 00:43:31,360
We're gonna...
